<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amanirenas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 40–10 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amanirenas was a ruling queen, or kandake, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kingdom of Kush</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a>. Around 25 BCE her armies attacked Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, sacking Syene and carrying off statues of Augustus, one of whose bronze heads was buried at Meroë. After Roman counter-attacks, a peace concluded with Augustus at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> around 21 BCE remitted tribute and was favourable to Kush.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amanirenas.jpg" alt="Amanirenas" width="300" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karl Richard Lepsius (1810-84) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bar_4_Kush_queen_(Lepsius).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Amanirenas, also spelled Amanirena, was queen regnant of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kingdom of Kush</a> in the mid to late 1st century BCE. Her full title was Amnirense qore li kdwe li, 'Amanirenas, qore and kandake'. Meroitic, the language of Kush, remains undeciphered, but inscriptions giving her both 'qore' and 'kandake' suggest that she ruled in her own right. Her name appears on stelae beside those of Teriteqas and Akinidad. Their relationship is not entirely clear, but scholars generally consider Akinidad her son and Teriteqas her husband, with kandake her title.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>According to the geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, whose account is the main source, Kushite forces attacked Thebais and Syene in 25 BCE while the prefect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aelius-gallus">Aelius Gallus</a> was away on his expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. They took Syene, Elephantine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a>, enslaved inhabitants and destroyed statues of Augustus. Strabo calls the queen Candace, a Latinized form of her title, and describes her as blind in one eye. The war lasted three years, from 25 to 22 BCE.</p><p>Gaius Petronius, the new prefect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, marched against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushites</a> at Syene with fewer than ten thousand infantry and eight hundred cavalry, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, facing thirty thousand men armed with ox-hide shields, axes, pikes and swords. The Kushites were overwhelmed and fled, and their generals were captured and sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. Petronius then took Pselchis and Premnis. Amanirenas, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a> with her son, offered to return the captives and the statues, but her envoys were ignored and the city was razed. She attacked Premnis a second time, but Petronius reached it first. Her ambassadors were then taken to Augustus at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a>, who imposed no further tribute.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The settlement moved the Roman frontier south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s former limit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qasr-ibrim">Primis</a>, now Qasr Ibrim. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> evacuated part of the Thirty-Mile Strip, the Meroites were exempt from tribute and the Romans kept the Dodekashoinos as a military border zone. The treaty remained in force until the end of the third century CE.</p><p>A bronze head of Augustus, larger than life, with eyes of calcite, metal rings and glass, was found by the archaeologist John Garstang in 1910 buried beneath the steps of a temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a> and was given to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> in 1911. The Hamadab Stela, found by Garstang in 1914, names Amanirenas and Akinidad and shows shackled prisoners beneath the ruling couple. It contains the Meroitic term Areme, which may mean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and scholars think it may give a Kushite account of the campaign.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amanirenas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Royal women held exceptional influence: queen mothers had a recognised part in the succession, and several queens, known as kandakes, ruled in their own right, among them <b>Amanirenas</b>, who confronted Rome, and Amanishakheto.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
